        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d6894a90-8c9b-574a-a3b5-df817fc5377b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: James Brown, Bobby Byrd, Baby Lloyd Stallworth\n- Songs: \"Lost Someone\"\n- Groups: The Famous Flames, Gospel Starlighters, the Avons, the Five Royals, the Flames\n- Topics: soul music, funk music\n\nFacts:\n- Bobby Howard Byrd was born on August 15, 1934, and he later became an American R&B and soul singer, songwriter, bandleader, talent scout, record producer, and musician.\n- Bobby Byrd began his career in 1952 as a member of the gospel group the Gospel Starlighters.\n- Bobby Byrd's group changed its name to the Avons in 1953.\n- Bobby Byrd's group changed its name to the Five Royals in 1954.\n- Bobby Byrd's group adopted the name the Flames in 1955 before James Brown joined, and their agent later changed the name to The Famous Flames.\n- Bobby Byrd played an integral part in the development of soul and funk music through his association with James Brown.\n- \"Lost Someone\" is a song recorded by James Brown in 1961.\n- \"Lost Someone\" was written by James Brown and Famous Flames members Bobby Byrd and Baby Lloyd Stallworth.\n- Bobby Howard Byrd died on September 12, 2007.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "1ed04839-1267-514b-94e3-0eb893ab13e1",
        "text": "Page: Lost Someone. Sentence: \"Lost Someone\" is a song recorded by James Brown in 1961.\nPage: Lost Someone. Sentence: It was written by Brown and Famous Flames members Bobby Byrd and Baby Lloyd Stallworth.\nPage: Bobby Byrd. Sentence: Bobby Howard Byrd (August 15, 1934 – September 12, 2007) was an American R&B/soul singer, songwriter, bandleader, talent scout, record producer, and musician, who played an integral and important part in the development of soul and funk music in association with James Brown.\nPage: Bobby Byrd. Sentence: Byrd began his career in 1952 as member of the gospel group the Gospel Starlighters, who later changed their name to the Avons in 1953 and the Five Royals in 1954, before settling with the name the Flames in 1955 prior to Brown's joining the group; their agent later changed it to The Famous Flames.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
